Yes — done, and verified against your live Okta tenant: nao now has a backend endpoint that starts the flow server-side, so the login page is never rendered.

## What to change in Okta

In the `nao-local` app under **General Settings** → **Login**, set **Initiate login URI** to:

```
http://localhost:3000/api/sso/start
```

(and keep **Login initiated by** = `Either Okta or App` with **Display application icon to users** checked). Clicking the tile now goes tile → Okta authorize → nao, already signed in.

## How it works

`GET /api/sso/start` validates the `iss` param against your configured provider, asks better-auth for an authorization URL, forwards the state/PKCE cookies, and 302s the browser to Okta:

```19:40:apps/backend/src/routes/sso.ts
	app.get('/sso/start', async (request, reply) => {
		const { iss } = request.query as { iss?: string };

		if (!isOidcConfigured() || !(await hasFeature(LICENSE_FEATURES.sso))) {
			return reply.redirect(LOGIN_PATH, 302);
		}
		if (iss && !isSameIssuer(iss, getOidcIssuer())) {
			app.log.warn({ iss }, 'Ignoring identity-provider-initiated sign-in from an unknown issuer');
			return reply.redirect(LOGIN_PATH, 302);
		}
		// ... start authorization, forward cookies, redirect
```

The authorization request is delegated to better-auth's own `sign-in/oauth2` endpoint rather than hand-rolled, so state and PKCE behave exactly as they do for the **Continue with Okta** button. The `iss` check compares origins because Okta sends the org domain (`https://integrator-4300145.okta.com`) while your discovery URL points at the `default` authorization server underneath it.

I verified with curl against your running backend:

| Request | Result |
|---|---|
| `?iss=https://integrator-4300145.okta.com` | 302 to Okta `/v1/authorize` with `code_challenge` + state cookie |
| `?iss=https://evil.example.com` | 302 to `/login` |
| no `iss` | 302 to Okta (direct visits still work) |

The `redirect_uri` it generates is `http://localhost:3000/api/auth/oauth2/callback/okta`, matching what you already registered.

## Also kept

The frontend fallback from the previous step still applies when the login page itself is loaded with an `iss` param, for deployments that point the initiate-login URI at `/login`. The issuer comparison lives in `@nao/shared/oidc` so both sides use one implementation, and the trpc OIDC config now exposes `issuer`.

Docs updated: `apps/backend/docs/auth-oidc.md`, the local testing guide (including the Federation Broker Mode gotcha you hit), and the public [Enterprise authentication page](nao-agent/enterprise/authentication.mdx).

Lint and typecheck pass across all three workspaces. Two failures remain in `auth-hooks-oidc.test.ts` around `isEmailDomainAllowed` with an empty allowlist, plus a batch of `bun:sqlite`/`better-sqlite3` suites that can't load under Node — both pre-existing and untouched by this change.

